Sure! I'll implement an email notification system. First, I'll set up the email configurations in `src/config.js`. Then, I'll modify `src/alerts.js` to include a function to send an email alert when a service status changes to down.

Here’s the snippet I will add to `src/config.js`:

```javascript
const emailSettings = {
  service: 'gmail',
  auth: {
    user: 'your-email@gmail.com',
    pass: 'your-password'
  }
};

module.exports = { emailSettings };
```

Next, in `src/alerts.js`, I’ll add the following function:

```javascript
const nodemailer = require('nodemailer');
const { emailSettings } = require('./config');

const transporter = nodemailer.createTransport(emailSettings);

const sendAlertEmail = async (serviceName) => {
  const mailOptions = {
    from: 'your-email@gmail.com',
    to: 'recipient@example.com',
    subject: 'Service Down Alert',
    text: `The service ${serviceName} is currently down!`
  };

  try {
    await transporter.sendMail(mailOptions);
    console.log('Alert email sent successfully.');
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error sending alert email:', error);
  }
};

module.exports = { sendAlertEmail };
```

Lastly, I’ll update the function that checks service status to include a call to `sendAlertEmail` when a service is detected as down. I’ll also write tests for this functionality in `tests/alerts.test.js`.
